::MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.9
Question:
A survey asks 30 students from one lunch table about the whole school. What is the main concern?
{
=A. sampling bias#Correct. One lunch table may not represent the whole school.
~B. causation#The issue is sample selection, not cause and effect.
~C. positive association#The question does not describe two related variables.
~D. residual error#Residuals belong to model predictions, not sampling design.
}
::MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.9
Question:
The population is all high school students in a district. Which sample is most representative?
{
~A. students from one honors class#One class may not represent all students.
=B. students randomly selected from all district high schools#Correct. Random selection across schools better represents the population.
~C. only students at a football game#This convenience sample may exclude many students.
~D. only students who volunteer online#Voluntary response can create bias.
}
::MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.9
Question:
Which survey question is biased?
{
~A. What time does school start?#This asks for factual information.
~B. Which schedule do you prefer?#This is more neutral than praising one option.
=C. Do you agree that the excellent new schedule should stay?#Correct. The wording pushes a positive opinion before the person answers.
~D. How many minutes is lunch?#This asks for a measurement, not an opinion.
}
::MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.9
Question:
A sample is chosen because it is easy to reach. What type of sample is this?
{
~A. random sample#A random sample is selected by chance.
~B. representative sample#Easy access does not guarantee representation.
~C. population#The population is the entire group, not the selected sample.
=D. convenience sample#Correct. A convenience sample is selected because it is easy to access.
}
::MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.9
Question:
Why should conclusions from a biased sample be limited?
{
~A. bias always makes arithmetic impossible#Calculations can be done, but conclusions may be misleading.
=B. the sample may not represent the population#Correct. If the sample is biased, the results may not generalize fairly.
~C. bias proves causation#Bias does not prove cause and effect.
~D. the population disappears#The population still exists; the sample may not represent it.
}
